+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration string into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d`, `h`, `m`, and `s`.
+Each unit may appear at most once and units must be given in that order, without spaces, for example `2d`, `1h30m`,
+or `1d2h3m4s`. Units are case-insensitive.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
